      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m wondering if the \"add\" annotation should be more precise. Most of the time, your tool knows that there is a jump at some callsite, that you can resolve to function[file:line], so I\u0027m wondering if the annotation should be made more explicit.\n\nExample:\n\none.c:\n1: void a() {\n2:     blah-\u003einit();\n3: }\n\nWe know that a[one.c:2] calls a function pointer, but we can\u0027t resolve it (so we should throw a warning).\n\nThen, the developer will be able to annotate the callsite, and say:\n\na[one.c:2]\n - my_sensor0_init\na[one.c:2]\n - my_sensor1_init\n\nIf either of my_sensor0_init or my_sensor1_init can be called.\n\nOf course that\u0027d require updating the annotations every time the code is changed, but maybe that\u0027s ok?\n\nWDYT?",
      "revId": "99941406f1e21ac9ab1e8b5c6286d893d3b1b506",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
